WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.940
In the first film, each old strand fixed its new partner.

2
00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:12.640
But how does a cell actually build those two new strands, one letter at a time?

3
00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:16.900
A real cell does not open the whole molecule at once.

4
00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:21.420
It opens the DNA at a moving Y, called the fork.

5
00:00:22.640 --> 00:00:25.780
The fork moves along, opening fresh template as it goes.

6
00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:30.660
Both old strands carry arrowheads, and they point opposite ways.

7
00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.760
The enzyme that builds new DNA is DNA polymerase.

8
00:00:36.880 --> 00:00:40.220
At each base, it picks the nucleotide that pairs with it.

9
00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:43.220
A wrong one almost never gets in.

10
00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:48.680
Then it joins that nucleotide to the growing strand with a covalent bond.

11
00:00:49.783 --> 00:00:51.923
Polymerase has one strict habit.

12
00:00:53.183 --> 00:00:56.603
It adds nucleotides only at the arrowhead end of a new strand.

13
00:00:58.023 --> 00:01:02.363
So every new strand grows arrowhead first, pointing the opposite way to its

14
00:01:02.363 --> 00:01:03.363
template.

15
00:01:03.833 --> 00:01:08.273
On the upper template, the new strand's arrowhead points the way the fork moves.

16
00:01:09.533 --> 00:01:14.873
So polymerase simply follows the fork, adding nucleotide after nucleotide in one

17
00:01:14.873 --> 00:01:15.873
long run.

18
00:01:16.533 --> 00:01:18.213
Now look at the lower template.

19
00:01:19.553 --> 00:01:22.973
Its new strand must point the other way, back away from the fork.

20
00:01:24.213 --> 00:01:28.113
If polymerase only adds at the arrowhead, how can it copy this strand?

21
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:39.830
It builds in short pieces, because the fork opens only a little template at a time.

22
00:01:41.130 --> 00:01:43.910
Each piece starts near the fork and grows away from it.

23
00:01:45.050 --> 00:01:49.790
When the fork opens more template, a fresh piece starts near the fork and grows back

24
00:01:49.790 --> 00:01:50.790
toward the last one.

25
00:01:51.970 --> 00:01:55.010
Another enzyme joins the pieces into one strand.

26
00:01:56.133 --> 00:02:00.353
Now zoom in on one step, because copying must also be accurate.

27
00:02:01.833 --> 00:02:05.393
Polymerase tests each incoming nucleotide against the template.

28
00:02:06.653 --> 00:02:09.993
A nucleotide that does not pair is usually turned away.

29
00:02:11.067 --> 00:02:13.547
Sometimes a wrong base slips in anyway.

30
00:02:14.947 --> 00:02:18.287
Polymerase checks each new base and removes most wrong ones.

31
00:02:19.567 --> 00:02:20.987
This step is called proofreading.

32
00:02:22.607 --> 00:02:25.827
Repair enzymes then catch many of the mistakes that slip past.

33
00:02:26.950 --> 00:02:30.770
Put the checks together: the matching check, proofreading, then repair.

34
00:02:32.150 --> 00:02:35.610
Each later check catches many mistakes the earlier ones missed.

35
00:02:36.910 --> 00:02:42.410
A textbook estimate is about one uncorrected mistake for every billion bases copied.

36
00:02:43.533 --> 00:02:45.333
So copying has two jobs.

37
00:02:46.733 --> 00:02:50.933
Pairing makes the copy possible, because it tells polymerase which base goes where.

38
00:02:52.313 --> 00:02:56.213
Checking makes the copy accurate, because it removes almost every slip.
